/-!
# Constructibility for compact support on a relative affine line

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0GKZ, lemma-constructible-shriek-rel-dim-1
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-etale-affine-line-shriek-sheaf-constructible).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Compactly supported cohomology of a constructible module sheaf on the relative affine
line is constructible, for every degree and every compactification.
Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0GKZ (finite commutative coefficient ring specialization).
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-etale-affine-line-shriek-sheaf-constructible)

Proof plan:

1. Write $A$ as the directed union of its subrings $A_i$ generated by finite subsets as rings. The
schemes $X_i=\operatorname{Spec}(A_i[t])$ have affine transition morphisms and limit
$\operatorname{Spec}(A[t])=\mathbf A^1_Y$: a map out of the union of the rings is exactly a
compatible family of ring maps, which proves this limit assertion directly. Apply constructible-
module limit descent to obtain $F_i$ constructible on $X_i$ with pullback $F$. Put
$Y_i=\operatorname{Spec}A_i$, $a_i:X_i\to Y_i$, and choose a compactification $c_i$ of $a_i$ using
nonemptiness of the compactification category. Its base change is a compactification of $a$. By
compact-support base change, exactness of module inverse image, and compactification independence,
for the compactification $c$ in the statement one has $\mathcal H^q(Ra_!^cF)\cong (Y\to
Y_i)^{-1}\mathcal H^q(Ra_{i,!}^{c_i}F_i)$. Pullback preserves constructibility (apply the
constructible-pullback theorem with module coefficients). Hence it suffices to treat affine bases
with finitely generated integer coordinate ring. Such schemes and their finite-type schemes are
Noetherian by the finite-type-over-Noetherian theorem. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-constructible-module-
limit-descent, lem-weil-pbc-etale-compact-support-base-change-qcqs, prop-weil-pbc-inverse-image-
modules-exact, lem-weil-pbc-etale-shriek-compactification-independence, prop-constructible-
pullback, lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian, def-weil-pbc-projection-compactification,
prop-nagata-compactification-category.

2. Fix such an affine $Y$. Use Noetherian induction on its closed subsets: for a closed subscheme
$B\hookrightarrow Y$, consider the assertion for every constructible module sheaf on $\mathbf
A^1_B$ and every compactification of its projection. Closed subschemes have finitely generated
integer coordinate rings by taking quotients. Whenever an open cover is needed, choose finitely
many principal affine opens inside the open subset; their coordinate rings are localizations
$R_s$, generated over $\mathbf Z$ by the generators of $R$ and $s^{-1}$. It is enough to prove the
assertion on a dense open of every nonempty closed $B$, since the complement is a strictly smaller
closed subset. The induction starts with the empty scheme, where all sheaves and their direct
images are zero. For a finite open cover constructibility is checked by refining the finitely many
local strata to a finite locally closed partition, then taking their finite locally constant
restrictions; this is directly the constructibility definition. For the union of a constructible
open $V$ and its closed complement the same conclusion follows by concatenating their
stratifications. Base change and exact inverse image identify restrictions of the output sheaves
with the output for the restricted affine-line projections. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-etale-compact-
support-base-change-qcqs, prop-weil-pbc-inverse-image-modules-exact, def-weil-pbc-etale-
constructible-sheaf.

3. For a nonempty closed affine base $B$, apply generic constancy with $\Lambda$-module
coefficients to the constructible sheaf $F$ on $\mathbf A^1_B$. It gives a dense open $U$ on which
$F$ is finite locally constant as a module sheaf. This uses the cited theorem rather than assuming
that an open with image inside a stratum factors through its chosen immersion. A dense open
contains every generic point of the finitely many irreducible components: its intersection with
each component off the other components is a nonempty open of that irreducible component, hence
contains its generic point. Let $Z$ be the reduced closed complement. Over each generic point
$\eta$ of $B$, the fiber $\mathbf A^1_{\kappa(\eta)}$ has its generic point in $U$ (this is the
generic point of the corresponding irreducible component of $\mathbf A^1_B$); its proper closed
subset $Z_\eta$ is finite, since the primes of $\kappa(\eta)[t]$ containing a nonzero polynomial
are its finitely many irreducible factors. The generic-finiteness neighborhood theorem applied to
$Z\to B$ gives a dense open $V\subseteq B$ on which $Z\to B$ is finite. Here $Z\to B$ is quasi-
compact, quasi-separated and of finite type because it is closed in the relative affine line over
a Noetherian base. Cover $V$ by finitely many principal affine opens and work on each of them.
Uses: def-weil-pbc-etale-constructible-sheaf, lem-weil-pbc-generic-finite-morphism-open-
neighborhood, prop-constructible-generic-constancy.

4. On each affine open $W$ of $V$, put $U_W=U\times_BW$ and $Z_W=Z\times_BW$. Restrictions of $F$
to these are constructible by constructible pullback; $F|_{U_W}$ is finite locally constant by the
choice of $U$. The lisse-relative-line input proves that $R(U_W\to W)_!(F|_{U_W})$ has
constructible cohomology. The map $Z_W\to W$ is finite and of finite presentation: on affine
charts its finite algebra is finitely generated and the base is Noetherian, so a finite algebra
presentation has finitely generated relation ideal. It is separated; on affine charts the finite-
ring-map quasi-finiteness theorem gives quasi-finiteness at every point, which is quasi-finiteness
of this finite-type morphism by the quasi-finite definition. Hence quasi-finite shriek
constructibility proves the same assertion for $R(Z_W\to W)_!(F|_{Z_W})$. No invertibility of the
order of $\Lambda$ is used. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-etale-lisse-open-relative-line-constructibility,
lem-weil-pbc-etale-quasifinite-shriek-constructible, prop-constructible-pullback, lem-weil-pbc-
finite-type-noetherian-finite-presentation, lem-weil-pbc-finite-ring-map-quasi-finite, pqf-def-
quasifinite.

5. Apply relative open-closed localization to $U_W\subseteq\mathbf A^1_W$ and its reduced closed
complement $Z_W$. In its cohomology exact sequence write $A^q=\mathcal H^q(R(U_W\to
W)_!F|_{U_W})$, $M^q=\mathcal H^q(R(\mathbf A^1_W\to W)_!F|_{\mathbf A^1_W})$, and $C^q=\mathcal
H^q(R(Z_W\to W)_!F|_{Z_W})$. Exactness gives $0\to\operatorname{coker}(C^{q-1}\to A^q)\to
M^q\to\ker(C^q\to A^{q+1})\to0$. The outer terms are constructible by kernels and cokernels
closure, and $M^q$ is constructible by extension closure. Thus all output cohomology is
constructible on $V$. The induction hypothesis applies to the closed complement $B\setminus V$,
with its reduced structure; base change identifies its output with the restriction of the original
output. Concatenating the open and closed stratifications gives constructibility on $B$. This
completes the induction; step 1 then returns to arbitrary $A$, and compactification independence
gives the assertion for every $c$. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-etale-compact-support-open-closed-triangle,
thm-constructible-serre, lem-weil-pbc-etale-compact-support-base-change-qcqs, prop-weil-pbc-
inverse-image-modules-exact, def-weil-pbc-etale-constructible-sheaf, lem-weil-pbc-etale-shriek-
compactification-independence.
-/
theorem isConstructibleEtaleModuleSheaf_affineLine_shriek
    (Λ : Type (u + 1)) [CommRing Λ] [Finite Λ] (Y : Scheme.{u}) [IsAffine Y]
    (F : Sheaf (AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin 1)) Y).smallEtaleTopology
      (ModuleCat.{u + 1} Λ)) (hF : Scheme.IsConstructibleEtaleModuleSheaf F)
    (c : Compactification (AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin 1)) Y ↘ Y)) (q : ℤ) :
    Scheme.IsConstructibleEtaleModuleSheaf
      ((_root_.DerivedCategory.Plus.homologyFunctor
        (Sheaf Y.smallEtaleTopology (ModuleCat.{u + 1} Λ)) q).obj
        ((Etale.rightDerivedLowerShriekOfCompactificationPlus (ModuleCat.{u + 1} Λ) c).obj
          ((_root_.DerivedCategory.Plus.singleFunctor
            (Sheaf (AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin 1)) Y).smallEtaleTopology
              (ModuleCat.{u + 1} Λ)) 0).obj F))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
